# Java 21 - Language and API Usage

The project targets **JDK 21** (`maven.compiler.release=21`). Use the newest **final** language
features; never enable preview features. Everything below is standard in 21.

## Records

Use a record whenever the type is a transparent, immutable carrier of data.

```java
public record Chromosome(float[] genes) {
  public Chromosome {
    genes = genes.clone(); // defensive copy; arrays are mutable
  }

  @Override
  public float[] genes() {
    return genes.clone(); // never leak the internal array
  }
}
```

Rules:

- Compact canonical constructors for validation and defensive copies.
- Never expose a mutable array or collection directly; clone on the way in and on the way out, or
  wrap with `List.copyOf` / `Map.copyOf` / `Set.copyOf`.
- Records are `final` and implicitly extend `java.lang.Record`; they cannot extend a class. Compose
  instead.
- A record with a state machine or identity (the `Bird` in a live simulation) is usually **not** a
  record: keep it a class and make the *snapshots* records (`CollisionEvent`, `Statistics`).
- `@RecordBuilder` is the approved way to get a builder or `with…` methods for a record that needs
  Jackson deserialisation or fluent copies. Do not hand-write a builder for a record.

## Sealed hierarchies

Use `sealed` when the set of subtypes is genuinely closed and the compiler should enumerate them.

```java
public sealed interface BrainOutput permits SpeedAdjustment, RotationAdjustment {}
```

- Every permitted subtype is `final`, `sealed` or `non-sealed`; state which and why if it is not
  `final`.
- Prefer a sealed interface over an enum when the cases carry different data; prefer an enum when
  they do not.
- Do not seal a type only to "document intent"; seal it when it buys exhaustive `switch`.

## Pattern matching and `switch`

```java
String describe(Individual individual) {
  return switch (individual) {
    case BirdIndividual bird -> "bird " + bird.getFitness();
    case NeuralNetworkIndividual network -> "network " + network.getFitness();
    default -> "unknown";
  };
}
```

- Pattern-matching `switch` over a sealed hierarchy needs no `default`; let the compiler tell you
  when a subtype is added.
- Record patterns deconstruct without accessor noise: `case CollisionEvent(Vector2D position, var rest)`.
- Use `instanceof` patterns (`if (obj instanceof Bird bird)`), never a cast after a check.
- Guarded patterns (`when`) are final in 21 — use them instead of a nested `if` inside a case.

## `Math.clamp` and numeric hygiene

`Math.clamp(value, min, max)` exists for `int`, `long`, `double` and `float` in 21. Replace every
nested `Math.min(max, Math.max(min, x))`.

```java
speed = Math.clamp(speed + adjustment, config.getSimSpeedMin(), config.getSimSpeedMax());
```

- The Rust `f32::clamp` panics when `min > max`; Java throws `IllegalArgumentException`. Keep the
  same contract and let it throw rather than silently returning garbage.
- Simulation state is `float` (matching Rust `f32`). Do not mix `double` maths into per-tick hot
  paths without converting back explicitly: a silent widening changes rounding and breaks golden
  tests.
- Distance comparisons use the squared distance (`Vector2D#distanceSquared`) unless the actual
  distance is needed; this avoids a `sqrt` per bird per food per tick.

## Sequenced Collections

`List`, `Deque` and `SortedSet` implement `SequencedCollection` in 21.

- `list.getFirst()` / `list.getLast()` instead of `list.get(0)` / `list.get(list.size() - 1)`.
- `list.reversed()` for a read-only reverse view instead of copying.
- `SequencedMap` (`putFirst`, `putLast`, `firstEntry`) for ordered maps.

## `var`, text blocks, `instanceof`

- `var` for locals when the right-hand side makes the type obvious (`var birds = world.getBirds()`).
  Never for fields, method parameters, or a right-hand side that hides an important type.
- Text blocks for JSON, SQL and multi-line log formats; `formatted(...)` for interpolation.
- `switch` expressions for pure mappings; `switch` statements for side effects.

## Streams and collections

- `stream().toList()` (unmodifiable, allows nulls) or `.collect(Collectors.toCollection(ArrayList::new))`
  when mutability is required.
- `List.copyOf`, `Map.copyOf`, `Set.copyOf` for defensive immutability; they reject nulls — that is
  a feature, not a bug to work around.
- Prefer an indexed `for` loop over a stream in the per-tick simulation path: allocation-free loops
  matter at 2500 steps × 40 birds × 60 foods.
- `Objects.requireNonNull(value, "message")` at boundaries; internal code relies on non-null by
  construction.

## Concurrency

- The domain is single-threaded per simulation and owns a `Random` per instance; never share a
  `Random` across threads (use `SplittableRandom` or a `ThreadLocalRandom` in the adapter).
- The optimizer parallelises whole simulations with `parallelStream` / a fixed pool; each task
  creates its own `Simulation` and `Random`. No shared mutable domain state.
- Virtual threads are appropriate for blocking I/O adapters (file writing, socket work), not for
  CPU-bound simulation work.
- JavaFX work stays on the FX application thread; `Platform.runLater` is the only bridge from a
  background thread, and it belongs in the UI adapter.

## Deprecations and warnings

- No deprecated API in new code. When touching a file, replace the deprecated call in the code you
  are already editing.
- Keep `-Xlint:deprecation,unchecked` at zero new warnings. An unchecked cast must be justified with
  a comment and, where possible, removed with a sealed type or generics.

## Anti-patterns to reject

- `new Random()` inside a domain method (non-deterministic, untestable).
- `System.out.println` in the domain (use SLF4J; adapters may print deliberate CLI output).
- `Thread.sleep` for animation (use JavaFX `Timeline` / `AnimationTimer`).
- Returning a mutable internal array from a getter (SpotBugs `EI_EXPOSE_REP`).
- `double` arithmetic silently replacing `float` in simulation hot paths.
- `Optional` as a field or parameter type; use it only as a return type.
- Checked exceptions leaking out of the domain; convert to `IllegalArgumentException` /
  `IllegalStateException` with a message, or model failure as a sealed result type.
